      discovery: {
        problem:
          "You have an accurate graph of {{y = x^2 - 2x - 3}}, and you are not allowed to draw another curve — only straight lines. How could you use it to solve {{x^2 - 2x - 5 = 0}}? What about {{x^2 - 3x - 4 = 0}}?",
        idea:
          "Rearrange so the left side is the curve you already have. {{x^2 - 2x - 5 = 0}} is the same as {{x^2 - 2x - 3 = 2}}, so draw the line **y = 2** and read off where it crosses the curve. {{x^2 - 3x - 4 = 0}} is the same as {{x^2 - 2x - 3 = x + 1}}, so draw **y = x + 1**. The x-coordinates of the crossing points are the solutions.",
      },
      body:
        "**Roots are x-intercepts.** The solutions of {{ax^2 + bx + c = 0}} are the x-coordinates where {{y = ax^2 + bx + c}} crosses the x-axis (the line y = 0).\n\n**More generally:** the solutions of f(x) = g(x) are the **x-coordinates of the intersections** of y = f(x) and y = g(x). That one idea covers everything:\n\n- f(x) = 0 → intersections with the x-axis;\n- f(x) = k → intersections with the horizontal line y = k;\n- f(x) = mx + c → intersections with a straight line.\n\n**Which line do I draw?** Exam questions give you a graph of one curve and an equation that looks different. Make the equation say **[the curve you have] = [something]**, then draw y = [something].\n\nOn the graph of {{y = x^2 - 2x - 3}}, to solve {{x^2 - 4x + 1 = 0}}:\n\n    {{x^2 - 4x + 1 = 0}}\n    {{x^2 - 2x - 3 - 2x + 4 = 0}}   (add and subtract to rebuild the curve)\n    {{x^2 - 2x - 3 = 2x - 4}}\n\nSo draw y = 2x − 4. A quick way to find the line: **(curve) − (equation)**, i.e. {{(x^2 - 2x - 3) - (x^2 - 4x + 1) = 2x - 4}}.\n\nThe same works for cubics. On the graph of {{y = x^3 - 3x}}, to solve {{x^3 - 5x + 1 = 0}}: {{x^3 - 3x = 2x - 1}}, so draw y = 2x − 1.\n\n**Simultaneous equations graphically.** For a linear and a quadratic (or cubic) equation, draw both; each intersection point gives one solution pair (x, y). Two crossings → two pairs; the line just touching the curve (a tangent) → one repeated solution; no crossings → no real solutions.\n\n**How many solutions?** Counting intersections is often the whole question. For {{x^3 - 3x = k}}, the horizontal line y = k cuts the S-shaped curve three times if −2 < k < 2 (between the turning values), twice if k = ±2, and once otherwise.\n\n**Accuracy.** Graph readings are estimates — usually to 1 d.p. Draw lines with a ruler, read carefully, and where possible check by substituting back.",
